.TH DEX\-SIDECAR 1 "August 19, 2026" "dexd 0.1.0" "dexd manual"
.SH NAME
dex\-sidecar \- write and check the sidecar that binds a frame rate
to a video
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dex\-sidecar write
.I stream.265
.RB [ \-\-fps
.IR F ]
.RB [ \-\-out
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.br
.B dex\-sidecar check
.I sidecar.json stream.265
.SH DESCRIPTION
A raw HEVC stream contains compressed pictures and nothing else, so it carries
no frame rate. dex\-sidecar stores the rate beside the video in a sidecar: a
small JSON file with the frame rate and a checksum of the video's bytes. dexd
refuses to start when that file is missing, unreadable, or bound to different
bytes, so write a sidecar for every video before you copy it to a dex card.
.PP
.B dex\-sidecar write
creates the sidecar for a video.
.B dex\-sidecar check
parses an existing sidecar and confirms that its checksum still matches the
video on disk; run it after copying a video and its sidecar to the card.
.PP
Both commands use dexd's own code to read the sidecar and compute the checksum,
so the sidecar format has one implementation: a sidecar dex\-sidecar accepts is
one dexd can read. dexd also checks the video itself and its config at startup
\(em see
.BR dexd (1).
.B write
builds the file, reads it back with that code and re\-checks the checksum
before the file takes its final name, so a failure at that point writes
nothing.
.SH OPTIONS
The options belong to
.BR write .
.B check
takes no options: it names the sidecar and the video as its two arguments.
.TP
.BI \-\-fps " F"
The frame rate to bind, written as
.BR 30 ,
.B 29.97
or
.BR 30000/1001 .
Without it, dex\-sidecar reads the rate from the video with ffprobe. See
.B FRAME RATE
below.
.TP
.BI \-\-out " file"
Where to write the sidecar. The default is the video's path with
.B .json
appended. See
.B FILES
below.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Proceed past the two refusals
.B write
makes: replacing an existing sidecar, and a frame\-rate disagreement (see
.BR "FRAME RATE" ).
.SH FRAME RATE
dex\-sidecar accepts a frame rate between 1 and 1000 frames per second. Without
.BR \-\-fps ,
it runs ffprobe and takes the rate the video's own headers carry. It discards a
value outside that range, or one that is not a frame rate at all: with no
timing in the video, ffprobe answers with a number it uses internally for
timing. dex\-sidecar then refuses and asks for
.BR \-\-fps .
.PP
Timing in the headers is written by the encoder, and many encoders write none.
When there is no rate to read, or ffprobe is not installed,
.B \-\-fps
is required.
.PP
Given both a
.B \-\-fps
and a rate from the video, the two must agree within 0.02 frames per second.
Two spellings of one rate agree, so
.B 30000/1001
and
.B 29.97
pass; 3 against 30 does not. dex\-sidecar refuses a disagreement unless you pass
.BR \-\-force ;
with
.B \-\-force
it writes the
.B \-\-fps
value and warns, naming the rate ffprobe read. At the wrong rate the video
plays too fast or too slow for as long as it runs, and nothing reports an
error.
.SH OUTPUT
.B write
prints the sidecar it wrote on standard output, and the path it wrote to on
standard error:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
{"fps":"30","sha256":"<64 hex digits>","width":3840,"height":2160}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Width and height come from ffprobe. dexd does not need them to play the video;
when
.B display_mode
is
.B auto
it compares them with the modes the connector offers and warns in the system
log if the display cannot show that size. When ffprobe does not report the
size, dex\-sidecar leaves both out and says so on standard error.
.PP
.B check
prints one line on standard output:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
OK fps=30 sha256=<64 hex digits> width=3840 height=2160
.fi
.RE
.PP
Width and height print as
.B \-
when the sidecar does not carry them.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I stream.265
The raw HEVC stream. The checksum covers its bytes as they are on disk,
unmodified, which are the bytes the player reads at startup.
.TP
.I stream.265.json
The sidecar. dexd looks for this name next to the video and has no option to
change it, so if you wrote it elsewhere with
.BR \-\-out ,
copy it next to the video under this name before you put the card in the
player.
.B write
creates the file under a temporary name in the same directory and renames it
into place, so an interrupted run leaves no half\-written sidecar.
.TP
.I /opt/dex
The assets directory on the player \(em the part of the dex card you see when
you put it in a computer. The video, its sidecar and the exhibit config sit
here together.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
dex\-sidecar wrote the sidecar, or the sidecar and the video match.
.TP
.B 1
dex\-sidecar could not read the video or the sidecar, could not write the
sidecar, or verification failed.
.TP
.B 2
The command line was malformed, or dex\-sidecar refused the frame rate or an
existing sidecar.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Every message goes to standard error, prefixed
.BR error: ,
.B warning:
or
.BR note: .
The refusals, and what each one asks for (exit 2):
.TP
.B <file> already exists; pass \-\-force to replace it
The sidecar is already there. Pass
.B \-\-force
to replace it, or
.B \-\-out
to write elsewhere.
.TP
.B \-\-fps <F> is not a frame rate dexd can read
Write the rate as a positive whole number, a positive decimal, or a positive
fraction.
.TP
.B \-\-fps <F> disagrees with <D>, the rate ffprobe read
Drop
.B \-\-fps
to use the rate from the video, or pass
.B \-\-force
if the given rate is right and ffprobe is wrong.
.TP
.B no frame rate for <file>, and none could be read from the stream
The video carries no timing, or ffprobe is missing or could not read the file.
Pass
.BR \-\-fps .
.PP
The failures (exit 1):
.TP
.B not a file: <path> \fRor\fB <path> is empty
You gave
.B write
a directory, a device, or an empty file.
.TP
.B asset does not match its sidecar: sha256 <A> != sidecar <B>
The video or the sidecar is stale, wrong, or truncated. Copy the video again
and run
.BR "dex\-sidecar check" ,
or prepare the video again with
.BR "dex\-sidecar write" .
.TP
.B the sidecar just built was rejected by dexd's own parser
The input is in order; this is a defect in dex\-sidecar. Nothing is
written.
.PP
Anything dex\-sidecar cannot read or write is reported by name with the reason
from the operating system.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PATH
Searched for ffprobe, which dex\-sidecar runs to read the frame rate and the
size of the video. With no ffprobe on PATH,
.B \-\-fps
is required and the sidecar carries no width or height.
.SH NOTES
Run dex\-sidecar on the workstation where you prepare the video, not on the
player: it needs the video file and ffprobe.
.PP
Do not write a sidecar by hand. The checksum has to cover the bytes the player
reads and the frame rate has to be one dexd reads back unchanged;
.B write
checks both against dexd's own code before it puts the file in place.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dexd (1),
.BR dex\-exhibit\-apply (1),
.BR dex\-wait\-hdmi (1)
.PP
